WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.153
Local LLM load balancing means placing one stable endpoint in front of several independently running model-serving servers.

2
00:00:08.153 --> 00:00:15.239
Each backend still has its own runtime, model files, memory limits, network address, logs, and failure modes.

3
00:00:15.239 --> 00:00:18.897
The load balancer does not make those systems disappear.

4
00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:27.735
It decides which available backend should receive an incoming request, while clients continue to call one predictable service address.

5
00:00:27.735 --> 00:00:37.184
That is different from distributed inference, model parallelism, or a true cluster designed to split one inference job across multiple machines.

6
00:00:37.184 --> 00:00:40.765
A normal load balancer sends one request to one backend.

7
00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:48.613
Specialized distributed inference can divide work across compute resources, but it requires specific runtime support.

8
00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:56.842
In this course, the focus is practical routing across local inference endpoints, not combining machines into one larger model.

9
00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:00.881
The most important expectation is latency versus throughput.

10
00:01:00.881 --> 00:01:06.519
Latency is the delay for one request, such as time to first token and total response time.

11
00:01:06.519 --> 00:01:10.405
Throughput is how much work the service completes over time.

12
00:01:10.405 --> 00:01:22.673
Two servers can increase total throughput when several requests are active, but they usually do not make a single response twice as fast because one backend is still generating that response.

13
00:01:22.673 --> 00:01:33.874
Load balancing is useful for labs, coding assistants, chat UIs, batch jobs, and small team environments because it gives tools one endpoint while the backend pool can change.

14
00:01:33.874 --> 00:01:37.531
Used well, it improves availability and spreads demand.

15
00:01:37.531 --> 00:01:48.732
Used poorly, it can hide overload, route requests to incompatible models, break streaming behavior, or expose a sensitive local inference service without proper controls.
